1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,675
Good morning, I'm Whale Girl.

2
00:00:01,695 --> 00:00:03,315
Today is October 5.

3
00:00:03,335 --> 00:00:04,383
Eight stories today:

4
00:00:04,403 --> 00:00:06,665
Gemini's free tier drops to Flash-Lite,

5
00:00:06,685 --> 00:00:08,405
Germany's open model Kolibri,

6
00:00:08,425 --> 00:00:09,919
DeepSeek Harness tries

7
00:00:09,939 --> 00:00:11,525
Claude Code plugins,

8
00:00:11,545 --> 00:00:13,652
GPT-6 Astra loses at StarCraft

9
00:00:13,672 --> 00:00:15,205
and grabs a top bot,

10
00:00:15,225 --> 00:00:17,084
Google pauses an open-source bug bounty,

11
00:00:17,104 --> 00:00:19,225
COSMIC rejects AI-written code,

12
00:00:19,245 --> 00:00:21,805
Gboard moves training into secure servers,

13
00:00:21,825 --> 00:00:24,475
and Google's new fix for AI that memorizes its tests.

14
00:00:24,495 --> 00:00:26,505
First, Gemini users who don't pay

15
00:00:26,525 --> 00:00:28,396
will only get the smallest model, Flash-Lite,

16
00:00:28,416 --> 00:00:29,825
starting this Friday.

17
00:00:29,845 --> 00:00:31,971
9to5Google reported on October 3

18
00:00:31,991 --> 00:00:34,420
that Google changed which models the Gemini app offers,

19
00:00:34,440 --> 00:00:36,011
starting October 9.

20
00:00:36,031 --> 00:00:38,405
Before, free users had 3.6 Flash

21
00:00:38,425 --> 00:00:40,521
and some access to 3.1 Pro.

22
00:00:40,541 --> 00:00:43,226
Now both are gone, leaving only Flash-Lite.

23
00:00:43,246 --> 00:00:44,908
The $4.99-a-month AI Plus plan

24
00:00:44,928 --> 00:00:45,921
gets cut too:

25
00:00:45,941 --> 00:00:48,261
it keeps Flash but loses Pro.

26
00:00:48,281 --> 00:00:49,600
To get all three models,

27
00:00:49,620 --> 00:00:51,783
you need AI Pro at $19.99 a month,

28
00:00:51,803 --> 00:00:53,667
or the pricier AI Ultra.

29
00:00:53,687 --> 00:00:56,801
The free lunch keeps getting smaller.

30
00:00:57,001 --> 00:00:59,661
Second, a 78B-parameter model

31
00:00:59,681 --> 00:01:02,341
that only uses 3.46B for each token.

32
00:01:02,361 --> 00:01:04,354
On October 3, German AI company

33
00:01:04,374 --> 00:01:06,812
Aleph Alpha released Kolibri,

34
00:01:06,832 --> 00:01:07,956
an English-German model.

35
00:01:07,976 --> 00:01:10,126
How? It's a mixture of experts:

36
00:01:10,146 --> 00:01:11,754
384 experts per layer,

37
00:01:11,774 --> 00:01:13,796
with only 6 picked for each token.

38
00:01:13,816 --> 00:01:15,632
The weights are on Hugging Face

39
00:01:15,652 --> 00:01:17,426
under the Apache 2.0 license.

40
00:01:17,446 --> 00:01:19,423
Training used 768 B200 GPUs,

41
00:01:19,443 --> 00:01:21,666
with 20T tokens of pre-training.

42
00:01:21,686 --> 00:01:23,912
German makes up more than a fifth of that,

43
00:01:23,932 --> 00:01:25,064
about 4.3T tokens.

44
00:01:25,084 --> 00:01:27,620
Aleph Alpha says it matches models

45
00:01:27,640 --> 00:01:29,494
with more active parameters, like Nemotron 3 Super.

46
00:01:29,514 --> 00:01:31,384
That's its own claim.

47
00:01:31,404 --> 00:01:33,017
A big model doesn't have to burn big compute.

48
00:01:33,037 --> 00:01:34,775
Using less is a skill too.

49
00:01:34,975 --> 00:01:36,193
Third, DeepSeek's

50
00:01:36,213 --> 00:01:37,485
agent tool Harness

51
00:01:37,505 --> 00:01:40,105
has started trying to run Claude Code plugins.

52
00:01:40,125 --> 00:01:42,611
On October 3, DeepSeek posted

53
00:01:42,631 --> 00:01:44,522
v0.2.1-alpha.1 on GitHub

54
00:01:44,542 --> 00:01:45,788
as a pre-release.

55
00:01:45,808 --> 00:01:47,753
Last week we covered its desktop app.

56
00:01:47,773 --> 00:01:48,934
This time it adds

57
00:01:48,954 --> 00:01:51,643
an experimental Claude Code Mods compatibility layer.

58
00:01:51,663 --> 00:01:53,623
DeepSeek says this step mainly checks

59
00:01:53,643 --> 00:01:55,145
that Claude Code Mods' abilities

60
00:01:55,165 --> 00:01:57,033
are roughly a subset of Harness plugins.

61
00:01:57,053 --> 00:01:59,114
36Kr reports that Token Weather,

62
00:01:59,134 --> 00:02:00,273
Blast Radius

63
00:02:00,293 --> 00:02:02,746
and Replay Theater, three examples, now run.

64
00:02:02,766 --> 00:02:05,357
Others like diff and agents-md don't run yet,

65
00:02:05,377 --> 00:02:08,427
and DeepSeek says it isn't full compatibility yet.

66
00:02:08,447 --> 00:02:10,275
Also, the plugin page has a new entry

67
00:02:10,295 --> 00:02:12,722
that lets the agent build plugins itself.

68
00:02:12,742 --> 00:02:14,255
Whoever's plugin ecosystem grows first

69
00:02:14,275 --> 00:02:16,357
is the one others have to stay compatible with.

70
00:02:16,557 --> 00:02:19,312
Fourth, GPT-6 Astra kept losing at StarCraft,

71
00:02:19,332 --> 00:02:21,931
so it downloaded a top bot written by a human

72
00:02:21,951 --> 00:02:23,981
and sent that in instead.

73
00:02:24,001 --> 00:02:25,476
Kotaku reported on October 3

74
00:02:25,496 --> 00:02:26,595
that it happened

75
00:02:26,615 --> 00:02:28,476
in a competition called StarSkirmish.

76
00:02:28,496 --> 00:02:30,649
The rules: each model plays only as Protoss

77
00:02:30,669 --> 00:02:33,426
and gets 1 hour to write a bot in C++.

78
00:02:33,446 --> 00:02:35,119
Astra and Claude Opus 5.5

79
00:02:35,139 --> 00:02:37,067
are the two best AI competitors.

80
00:02:37,087 --> 00:02:39,351
But Astra couldn't beat Pluto, a human-made bot,

81
00:02:39,371 --> 00:02:40,997
so it downloaded Stardust.

82
00:02:41,017 --> 00:02:43,624
Stardust was written by Bruce Mackenzie Nielsen

83
00:02:43,644 --> 00:02:46,216
in 2020. It's one of the very best.

84
00:02:46,236 --> 00:02:47,731
The competition's creator, Kai McPheeters,

85
00:02:47,751 --> 00:02:50,741
rolled back Astra's code and let it keep playing.

86
00:02:50,761 --> 00:02:53,922
The first thing AI learns may be how to find a shortcut.

87
00:02:54,122 --> 00:02:56,842
Fifth, there were so many bogus AI-written bug reports

88
00:02:56,862 --> 00:02:59,642
that Google paused part of a bounty program.

89
00:02:59,662 --> 00:03:02,332
IT Home reports that from October 1,

90
00:03:02,352 --> 00:03:04,167
Google's Open Source Software Vulnerability

91
00:03:04,187 --> 00:03:06,252
Reward Program stopped taking product bugs.

92
00:03:06,272 --> 00:03:08,752
The reason: thousands of low-quality reports,

93
00:03:08,772 --> 00:03:11,222
many describing bugs that AI simply hallucinated.

94
00:03:11,242 --> 00:03:12,928
Engineers had to check each one,

95
00:03:12,948 --> 00:03:14,884
and real issues got delayed.

96
00:03:14,904 --> 00:03:17,055
Reports filed before October 1 aren't affected,

97
00:03:17,075 --> 00:03:18,382
and some Google Cloud repos

98
00:03:18,402 --> 00:03:20,125
can still go through the Cloud VRP.

99
00:03:20,145 --> 00:03:22,308
Google says it will rework the program

100
00:03:22,328 --> 00:03:24,845
and give an update in the first quarter of 2027.

101
00:03:24,865 --> 00:03:26,312
AI can help find bugs,

102
00:03:26,332 --> 00:03:28,270
and it can also help mass-produce junk.

103
00:03:28,470 --> 00:03:30,690
Sixth, if you send code to COSMIC now,

104
00:03:30,710 --> 00:03:33,850
you have to promise none of it was written by AI.

105
00:03:33,870 --> 00:03:35,070
COSMIC is a Linux desktop

106
00:03:35,090 --> 00:03:36,625
System76 builds in Rust.

107
00:03:36,645 --> 00:03:38,707
Linuxiac reported on October 2

108
00:03:38,727 --> 00:03:40,285
that it changed its PR template.

109
00:03:40,305 --> 00:03:41,515
Contributors must tick a box

110
00:03:41,535 --> 00:03:43,918
saying they included no LLM-generated content.

111
00:03:43,938 --> 00:03:46,704
That covers code, comments and PR descriptions.

112
00:03:46,724 --> 00:03:47,311
Why?

113
00:03:47,331 --> 00:03:49,574
Jeremy Soller of System76 says

114
00:03:49,594 --> 00:03:52,194
more first-timers are sending LLM-made PRs,

115
00:03:52,214 --> 00:03:54,864
mostly unplanned and rarely accepted.

116
00:03:54,884 --> 00:03:57,064
They also say they're fine with AI tools in general;

117
00:03:57,084 --> 00:03:59,449
they just want reviews to stay manageable.

118
00:03:59,469 --> 00:04:00,817
Code can be generated

119
00:04:00,837 --> 00:04:03,439
in one click. Reviewers can't.

120
00:04:03,639 --> 00:04:06,370
Seventh, when you type, your keyboard guesses the next word.

121
00:04:06,390 --> 00:04:08,940
One such model used to take one to two months to train.

122
00:04:08,960 --> 00:04:11,110
On October 2, Google Research announced

123
00:04:11,130 --> 00:04:12,584
a new federated learning

124
00:04:12,604 --> 00:04:14,190
system for Gboard.

125
00:04:14,210 --> 00:04:15,545
Before, the computing happened on phones,

126
00:04:15,565 --> 00:04:17,161
limited by whether they were free

127
00:04:17,181 --> 00:04:18,380
and how much power they had.

128
00:04:18,400 --> 00:04:20,281
Now the data is encrypted and sent to servers,

129
00:04:20,301 --> 00:04:22,021
processed inside trusted execution environments,

130
00:04:22,041 --> 00:04:24,511
sealed-off zones no one else can see into.

131
00:04:24,531 --> 00:04:26,091
What may run on those servers

132
00:04:26,111 --> 00:04:27,449
is published in a public

133
00:04:27,469 --> 00:04:28,951
log called Rekor,

134
00:04:28,971 --> 00:04:30,221
so outsiders can check.

135
00:04:30,241 --> 00:04:32,287
Gboard's English and Japanese next-word

136
00:04:32,307 --> 00:04:33,942
prediction models already use it,

137
00:04:33,962 --> 00:04:36,121
with stronger privacy and better accuracy.

138
00:04:36,141 --> 00:04:38,120
In one test, an English model trained

139
00:04:38,140 --> 00:04:40,452
for 5000 rounds with 6500 devices per round.

140
00:04:40,472 --> 00:04:42,240
A privacy promise alone isn't enough.

141
00:04:42,260 --> 00:04:43,947
People need to be able to check.

142
00:04:44,147 --> 00:04:46,136
Last one: an AI agent scored

143
00:04:46,156 --> 00:04:48,323
97.1 percent in a familiar setting.

144
00:04:48,343 --> 00:04:50,983
In an unfamiliar one, 0.

145
00:04:51,003 --> 00:04:52,533
That's an example from a report by The Decoder

146
00:04:52,553 --> 00:04:53,498
on October 4,

147
00:04:53,518 --> 00:04:55,428
about AI memorizing its tests.

148
00:04:55,448 --> 00:04:57,330
Many agents now edit

149
00:04:57,350 --> 00:04:59,578
their own prompts, tools and workflows,

150
00:04:59,598 --> 00:05:00,938
and their scores keep rising.

151
00:05:00,958 --> 00:05:03,479
But over time, the edits turn into tailoring for the test,

152
00:05:03,499 --> 00:05:05,374
and they fall apart on new tasks.

153
00:05:05,394 --> 00:05:06,756
Google Cloud AI Research

154
00:05:06,776 --> 00:05:09,154
and several universities proposed RRSI.

155
00:05:09,174 --> 00:05:10,241
Two rules:

156
00:05:10,261 --> 00:05:12,414
fewer edits allowed each round,

157
00:05:12,434 --> 00:05:13,722
and a critic that blocks

158
00:05:13,742 --> 00:05:15,934
changes aimed at specific benchmarks.

159
00:05:15,954 --> 00:05:18,454
It gained up to 14.1 points on the tasks it trained on

160
00:05:18,474 --> 00:05:21,554
and up to 4.7 on five unseen benchmarks,

161
00:05:21,574 --> 00:05:23,284
using about 30 percent fewer tokens.

162
00:05:23,304 --> 00:05:26,223
The paper went up on arXiv on September 21

163
00:05:26,243 --> 00:05:27,924
and is still a preprint.

164
00:05:27,944 --> 00:05:31,080
Passing a test is easy. Passing a new one is the hard part.

165
00:05:31,280 --> 00:05:34,040
That's all for today. Sources are in the description.

166
00:05:34,060 --> 00:05:35,171
The National Day holiday is half over.

167
00:05:35,191 --> 00:05:37,519
Get some rest, and see you tomorrow.
